# ---- Packaged deliverables (rebuilt from source, not source themselves) -------------------------
*.zip
dist/
build/

# ---- Web app (Vite/React, in web/) -------------------------------------------------------------
# node_modules is the actual source of "tons of files": a fresh React+Vite install is typically
# 20,000-40,000+ small files once every dependency's own dependencies are counted, none of which are
# yours to version - `npm install` rebuilds it exactly from package-lock.json on any machine. This is
# almost certainly what's overloading the repo if it ever got committed even once (git doesn't fully
# forget a large commit just because a later commit deletes the files - see the note at the bottom of
# this file if that's already happened).
web/node_modules/
node_modules/
# dist/ above already covers web/dist (Vite's build output), but these are its other caches/logs:
web/.vite/
.vite/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
*.tsbuildinfo

# ---- Mobile app (Expo/React Native, in mobile/) --------------------------------------------------
# Same story as web/node_modules - this is almost always the bulk of "tons of files" on the mobile
# side too, plus Expo/Metro's own caches and (if `expo prebuild` is ever run) the generated native
# android/ and ios/ projects, which are regenerable build output, not source, in a managed Expo app.
mobile/node_modules/
mobile/.expo/
.expo/
.expo-shared/
mobile/android/
mobile/ios/
web-build/
*.jsbundle
metro-cache/
.metro-health-check*

# ---- Editor / OS cruft --------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp

# ---- If node_modules (or anything else here) was already committed before this .gitignore --------
# Adding a pattern here only stops NEW files from being tracked - it does not untrack or shrink
# history for files already committed. If `git status` still shows node_modules/ as tracked after
# this, or your repo is already bloated from an earlier push, run this ONCE per folder to untrack it
# without deleting it from disk (then commit the removal):
#   git rm -r --cached web/node_modules mobile/node_modules
# That shrinks future clones, but the old, large commits still live in your repo's history/.git
# folder on GitHub. If the repo is painfully large even after that, the real fix is rewriting history
# with `git filter-repo` (or BFG Repo-Cleaner) to strip node_modules out of every past commit, then
# force-pushing - a more involved, one-time cleanup, worth doing only if GitHub is actually refusing
# pushes or the clone is unreasonably slow. Ask and we can walk through it together.